LOGBOOK.md-169-### 0851 — An NE lifecycle read from the SDK header alone was wrong in the direction that mattered (TASK-260728-7ii1xz, r2)
LOGBOOK.md-170-- ROOT CAUSE: r1 concluded "the app cannot reach a stopped provider" from the macOS 26.5 SDK header for `handleAppMessage:` — *"called by the framework when the container app sends a message to the provider"*. That sentence names **who sends**, not a liveness precondition, and the header for the sending side says nothing about launch at all. Apple's published API reference does: `sendProviderMessage(_:responseHandler:)` — *"If the extension is not running, **it should be launched to handle the message**."* One inference from a header that was silent, and revocation was declared permanently asynchronous, `379cpk` AC4 was declared unsatisfiable, and a 60-second startup budget was spent waiting for a seed. **Generalisable: for Network Extension lifecycle questions the shipped header and the published reference are not interchangeable — the header documents the signature, the reference documents the behaviour.** Check both before designing around an absence.
LOGBOOK.md:171:- DECISION: Revocation is now specified as a **five-state table**, not a yes/no. Synchronous when the configuration is installed and enabled (running or not — the send launches it); unavailable and deferred to a start-time sweep when the configuration is disabled (`NEVPNErrorConfigurationDisabled`), invalid/unapproved (`NEVPNErrorConfigurationInvalid`), or when no host is running at all. Seeding moved out of `startTunnel` entirely, so the credential is normally at rest before any start; `startTunnel` is now read-only and fails fast with `credentialNotProvisioned`. The start-time sweep survives as crash-recovery defence, not as the deletion path.
LOGBOOK.md-172-- FINDING: The reverse dependency stays forbidden and is what still kills the memory-only candidate B. Apple, same page: *"you should not rely on this communication channel with the containing app for basic Tunnel Provider operation (start, stop, etc.)."* App→provider may launch; provider→app may never be assumed. The transport selection (candidate D) therefore did not change — only the lifecycle around it.
LOGBOOK.md-173-- FINDING: `kSecUseKeychain` is **add-only**. `SecItem.h` defines it as "a value of type `SecKeychainRef` to which **`SecItemAdd`** will add the provided item(s)"; scoping a *search* takes `kSecMatchSearchList`. r1 specified `kSecUseKeychain` for the query. Verified end to end against a throwaway keychain — add/read/delete all `OSStatus=0`, and a **negative control** with the search list pointed at the user default returns `-25300 errSecItemNotFound`, proving the list actually scopes rather than being silently ignored. This also gives `379cpk`/`1o9wjz` a real unit-test seam needing neither root nor the System keychain.
LOGBOOK.md-174-- FINDING: Do not hard-code `/Library/Keychains/System.keychain`. `SecKeychainCopyDomainDefault(kSecPreferencesDomainSystem, &kc)` returns `OSStatus=0` and resolves to exactly that path from an ordinary user context (uid 502), and `SecKeychainCopyDomainSearchList` for the system domain has **count=1** — physically confirming TN3137's *"in the system context the search list includes just the System keychain"*. The fix is behaviour-preserving, so there was never a reason to pin the literal. The whole `SecKeychain`/`SecAccess` surface is `API_DEPRECATED(… macos(10.2, 10.10))` yet still ships in the 26.5 SDK and remains Apple's only documented daemon option.
LOGBOOK.md-175-- ANOMALY: `Security.framework/Headers/SecKeychain.h` is **ISO-8859 encoded**. A plain UTF-8 `grep` over it silently prints nothing and exits 1 — indistinguishable from "the symbol isn't there". This nearly produced the opposite error, concluding the domain APIs had been removed from the SDK. `iconv -f ISO-8859-1 -t UTF-8` first when grepping Apple headers; an empty grep result on a header is not evidence of absence.
LOGBOOK.md-176-- DECISION: A settled decision row must have its **pending scaffolding deleted with it**. The ypo7yo matrix row still carried `resolutionOwner: TASK-260728-7ii1xz` and an `amendmentRule` reading "if the transport gives the provider direct keychain access, amend to `required` with the group literal". The selected transport makes that antecedent **true** while the file-based keychain makes the consequent **false** — so leaving it in place was a live instruction to re-grant the row on precisely the reasoning the decision refutes. The amendment packet now covers all five keys on the row field by field. Worth a validator gate: a `prohibited` row that still names a `resolutionOwner` is a contradiction.
LOGBOOK.md-177-
LOGBOOK.md:178:### 0822 — macOS credential transport decided: the root provider owns a System keychain item, seeded once over the NE app-message channel (TASK-260728-7ii1xz)
LOGBOOK.md-179-- DECISION: Candidate D selected. Host reads the secret in user context, hands it to the running provider via `sendProviderMessage` → `handleAppMessage`, and the **root provider** persists it in the file-based **System keychain** (`/Library/Keychains/System.keychain`) with a restrictive `SecAccess`. This is exactly Apple DTS's recommendation for this shape (forums/thread/759976, /133933). Rejected: shared Data Protection Keychain group (cannot work), memory-only IPC seed (dies on system start), custom XPC Mach service (needs exception entitlements + makes OC-4 live), root-owned encrypted file (Tailscale's pattern — same security, more code, more entitlements).
LOGBOOK.md-180-- FINDING: The decisive fact is physically readable on this Mac. `/System/Library/Sandbox/Profiles/application.sb:792-793` (macOS 26.5/25F71, sha256 `2e711078…`): `(when (entitlement "com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension") (allow file-read* file-write* (subpath "/Library/Keychains")))`. An NE-entitled process already has System keychain access from the base App Sandbox. Lines 655-656 grant the `securityd`/`SecurityServer` Mach lookups to every sandboxed process. **The selected transport costs zero entitlements.**
LOGBOOK.md-181-- DECISION: `keychain-access-groups` on `works.relux.tunnel.mac.tunnel` — **keep prohibited**, promoted from `prohibited-pending-decision` to settled. Not "unnecessary" but *inapplicable*: the file-based keychain is ACL-controlled (`SecAccess`), access groups are a Data Protection Keychain concept only (TN3137). Applying owner is `TASK-260715-ypo7yo`; closes OC-1, downgrades OC-4.
LOGBOOK.md-182-- ANOMALY: Second instance of the pattern logged at 0755 — **shipping entitlements are not evidence of requirement**. Surfshark's provider ships a `/Library/Keychains/` temporary exception the current sandbox profile makes redundant; Surfshark and Tailscale both ship `/private/var/db/mds/` exceptions dating to a Catalina-era bug fixed in Big Sur (forums/thread/660123), which `application.sb:544-546` already covers for uid 0; and PureVPN's provider carries `keychain-access-groups` that Facts 1-2 say cannot bridge the user boundary. Three shipping products, three stale or inert entitlements.
LOGBOOK.md-183-- FINDING: **The App Group channel is broken on macOS too**, not just the keychain. `containerURL(forSecurityApplicationGroupIdentifier:)` resolves relative to the caller's home and the root provider's home is `/private/var/root` (verified root-private, `ls` exit 1). `.spec/architecture.md:111` says shared state is "an App Group configuration snapshot and Keychain references" — both halves fail on macOS. The non-secret snapshot must travel in `providerConfiguration`, which the accepted M1 lifecycle contract already does. Directly affects `TASK-260715-3f4lxy`.
LOGBOOK.md-184-- FINDING: One property macOS **cannot** offer that iOS does: the System keychain unlocks at boot from root-only `/private/var/db/SystemKey`, so the secret is **not** protected by the user's login password. ~~(2) deletion cannot be synchronous — the user-context app cannot delete a root-owned item while the provider is stopped, so revocation is a provider request reconciled by a start-time sweep.~~ **CORRECTED in r2 — see the 0851 entry.** Revocation *is* synchronous whenever the NE configuration is installed and enabled; the r1 claim rested on a misread SDK header.
LOGBOOK.md:185:- FINDING: System keychain **attributes are world-readable** — `security dump-keychain /Library/Keychains/System.keychain` as uid 502 exits 0 and prints 192 items' attributes. Item *data* is ACL-gated (`-d` blocked on an interactive authorization prompt, killed at 120s). Consequence: the credential item's service/account values must carry no hostname, account, or profile name.
LOGBOOK.md-186-- NOTE: Experiment design worth reusing. `ssh localhost` gives a **`Background`** launchd session at the same uid while the terminal is **`Aqua`** — a free, no-root way to isolate the "user login context" axis. It showed the file-based login keychain returning `OSStatus=0` in Aqua and `-25308 errSecInteractionNotAllowed` in Background. It could **not** test the Data Protection Keychain: an ad-hoc-signed CLI returns `-34018 errSecMissingEntitlement` in both contexts, because DP access groups must be authorized by a provisioning profile (TN3137) and Ceremony C1 has not run.
LOGBOOK.md-187-- BLOCKED: Nothing. Named limitation instead — `sudo -n true` exit 1 in a non-interactive session, so **no code was run as root**. Checks V1–V3 (V1 System keychain write from the signed sandboxed provider, V2 root group-container path, V3 system start with the app not running) were appended to `TASK-260715-9yp8to` rather than creating a duplicate physical-Mac node (ADR-028a). r2 added **V4/V5** to the same node.
