LOGBOOK.md-219-### 0657 — The macOS provider runs as root, so the specified Keychain sharing cannot work (TASK-260715-ypo7yo)
LOGBOOK.md-220-- BLOCKER: `.spec/security-privacy.md` requires private keys and passphrases to live in the Data Protection Keychain "shared only through the minimum keychain access group required by the app and its packet tunnel extension" (also `.spec/threat-model.md` DF-02/M-02). On macOS that is unsatisfiable. Developer ID direct distribution forces the provider to be a **system extension** (TN3134, `.spec/platform-distribution.md`), a sysex runs **as root** outside a user context, the Data Protection Keychain is available only to code in a user context, and keychain access groups share items between programs running as the **same** user. The host runs as the logged-in user. iOS is unaffected — its appex runs as the same user as its host.
LOGBOOK.md-221-- FINDING: The same root/user split kills the App Group **container** as a macOS host↔provider channel: the sysex's container resolves under `/var/root/Library/Group Containers`, the host's under the user's home. App Groups are still **required** on macOS, but only as the XPC Mach-service namespace a sandboxed process must prefix with — not as shared storage. `TASK-260715-3f4lxy` (profile snapshot loader) inherits this.
LOGBOOK.md:222:- DECISION: The matrix withholds `keychain-access-groups` from `works.relux.tunnel.mac.tunnel` (`prohibited-pending-decision`) rather than provisioning a right the provider cannot exercise. Created `TASK-260728-7ii1xz` to select the macOS credential transport and return a grant-or-keep-prohibited amendment; it blocks `379cpk`, `1o9wjz`, `29ws8l`, `3f4lxy`. Five credential tasks all assumed the shared-group model and none owned the question of whether it holds.
LOGBOOK.md-223-- NOTE: The trap that made this invisible is that the entitlement is *accepted* everywhere — portal, profile, and `codesign` all succeed. Only a runtime `SecItem` call in the provider fails, which is M1-deep. Least privilege caught it because "grant only what the target can actually use" forced the question.
