LOGBOOK.md-847-- FINDING: During any KEX (client- or server-initiated) upstream's state machine rejects outbound `channelData` with `protocolViolation` (`SSHConnectionStateMachine.swift:1060-1076`), failing the child write promise. Pre-existing upstream behavior, unchanged by the fork — but automatic byte/time rekey makes the collision window likely under bulk traffic. The NIOSSH adapter (TASK-260715-1af33i) MUST gate/queue child writes on `rekeySnapshot.keyExchangeInProgress` / `NIOSSHRekeyStartedEvent`→`NIOSSHRekeySucceededEvent`; the fork exposes exactly this observability for the contract's "writes MAY suspend" clause.
LOGBOOK.md-848-- FINDING: `NIOSSHChannelWindowConfiguration` invariant is `remaining + buffered + delivered == initialWindowSize`, so remaining credit never grows past `initialWindowSize` even when `maximumWindowSize` is larger, and a `windowAdjustmentThreshold > initialWindowSize` (allowed by preconditions, which only check ≤ max) permanently withholds WINDOW_ADJUST — a stalled-channel footgun. Adapter policy must keep threshold ≤ initial (≤ half is sane). Cap semantics remain contract-correct: `remainingBefore + adjustment ≤ cap` always holds.
LOGBOOK.md:849:- NOTE: `.serverInitiated` rekey reason semantically means "remote-initiated" (a server-role handler labels client-triggered KEX the same way); cosmetic for the client-only tunnel. `requestRekey` in a non-active state fails the promise and also fires `errorCaught` — callers should not treat pre-active requests as recoverable no-ops.
